{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 199,
    "dDurationMs": 3911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What happens if an immovable\nobject meets an unstoppable force? Is a popular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4110,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "question on the internet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5590,
    "dDurationMs": 3919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, relativity clearly tells us that\nthere is no such thing as an immovable object"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9509,
    "dDurationMs": 5001,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- here's why: If you pick any supposedly immovable\nobject, or just something like your house"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14510,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the earth, I can make it move. All I need\nto do is start to move relative to it, for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19630,
    "dDurationMs": 4399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example I might ride a rocket, and suddenly\nfrom my perspective, I'm not moving and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24029,
    "dDurationMs": 5431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earth sails by outside. The laws of physics\nmake no preference for inertial frame of reference,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29460,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so from my perspective here, I do not stir,\nand yet it's clear: the immovable object moves!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35480,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So because of relativity, \"immovable objects\"\ncannot be. But what I think people normally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MEAN by \"immovable object\" is something that\nif it's not moving, you can't make it start"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44040,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving by Pushing on it. So, not an immovable\nobject, but an \"un-acceleratable\" one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48870,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Using Newton's second law, we know that an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50810,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object's acceleration is equal to the total\nforce on it divided by its mass (though you've"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54920,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably seen this as F=ma). So an \"un-acceleratable\"\nobject would be an object with infinite mass;"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60820,
    "dDurationMs": 4399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an object so massive that no matter how big\nthe total force F is, when you divide F by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65219,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "m you always get zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67179,
    "dDurationMs": 3711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, as we've said, not being able to\naccelerate an object doesn't necessarily mean"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70890,
    "dDurationMs": 4009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the object isn't moving - it just means\nthat you can't directly change its speed - if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74899,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's not moving, then it'll stay not moving.\nAnd if it's moving at 100 miles an hour, then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78799,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it'll stay moving at 100 miles per hour."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81049,
    "dDurationMs": 4581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what about an unstoppable force? Well,\nall the fundamental forces in nature are actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85630,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "caused by particles (like photons or gluons\nor gravitons) that interact with an object"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89670,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and change its momentum - the only way to\nNOT be affected by a force is to not interact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93670,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with it at all (like how electrons don't interact\nwith gluons so they aren't subject to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97380,
    "dDurationMs": 4849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strong nuclear force). Even light itself is\nan unstoppable force - every photon that hits"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102229,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your body changes your momentum a tiny little\nbit, and there's nothing you can do about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105749,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it other than avoid light altogether or become\ntransparent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108739,
    "dDurationMs": 3150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So all forces are already unstoppable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111889,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but my impression is that the phrase \"unstoppable\nforce\" isn't really meant to imply anything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115639,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about \"forces\" like electromagnetism or gravity,\nbut rather, something that you cannot stop"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119679,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from barreling down on you. That is, an object\nwhose velocity cannot be changed by pushing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124539,
    "dDurationMs": 490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125029,
    "dDurationMs": 3971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, IF by an unstoppable force we mean an\nobject moving at a speed that can never be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129000,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changed, then that means the object cannot\naccelerate - but wait a second, this sounds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133380,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "familiar! Recalling what we learned earlier,\nan unstoppable force must be an un-acceleratable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137860,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object! And that means that an \"unstoppable\nforce\" and an \"immovable object\" are really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142320,
    "dDurationMs": 2730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just the same, viewed from different reference\nframes!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145050,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, since infinite mass requires infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147530,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy, we don't know of anything in the universe\nthat behaves like this, not the least because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151640,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would automatically be a black hole so\nbig that everything in the universe would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154610,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "already be inside of it. But what if we\nignored gravity and imagined there WERE an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158570,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "un-acceleratable object? Well, first it would\nbe a source of infinite free power and would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162550,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allow us to live in a 100% happy utopian society\nand break the second law of thermodynamics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166610,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and probably create portals and time travel,\ntoo (you can do a lot with infinite energy)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171080,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But more importantly, if two of these infinitely\nmassive un-acceleratable objects were moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175140,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards each other and collided, then since\nby definition it's not possible for the velocity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179720,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of either of them to change, the only possibility\nis that they must pass right through each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183650,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other with no effect on each other at all."
    } ]
  } ]
}
